      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Excretion – Concept and Importance"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Excretion removes metabolic nitrogenous wastes (ammonia, urea, and uric acid) to maintain homeostasis and osmoregulation.",
        "The human excretory system comprises a pair of kidneys, ureters, a urinary bladder, and a urethra.",
        "The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney, divided into the renal corpuscle (glomerulus and Bowman's capsule) and renal tubule.",
        "Urine formation occurs through three sequential processes: Glomerular Ultrafiltration, Selective Reabsorption, and Tubular Secretion.",
        "Hormones like Anti-Diuretic Hormone (ADH), Aldosterone (via RAAS pathway), and Atrial Natriuretic Factor (ANF) strictly regulate kidney function.",
        "Renal disorders include kidney stones (renal calculi), uremia, glomerulonephritis, and end-stage renal disease treated via hemodialysis or kidney transplantation."
      ],
      "id": "43j913r"
    },
